PROCESS  (9 phases, no shortcuts, no skips)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

PHASE 0 — FIX LOG ARCHAEOLOGY (mandatory first step)
  Read the entire FIX LOG comment block at the top of web-api.js.
  For each listed fix: understand WHY it was needed, what pattern
  it exemplifies, and whether a sister path with the same root cause
  still exists anywhere in the codebase. Create a mental model of
  which bugs have appeared more than once.

PHASE 1 — ARCHITECTURE MAP
  Map every module, every event-bus channel name (exact string —
  typos make emits invisible to listeners), every localStorage key
  (with sas: prefix accounting), every async flow, every shared
  mutable variable, and every piece of state that crosses a module
  boundary. Identify which state is ephemeral (memory) and which
  is persistent (localStorage). Identify which state must survive
  a page reload and which should not.

PHASE 2 — STATIC ANALYSIS (line by line, every file)
  Read every line of every file. Apply the full audit checklist.
  Apply all five lenses to every significant function. Apply the
  13 bug pattern checks (A through M). Do NOT start fixing yet —
  catalogue everything first so fixes don't mask each other.

PHASE 3 — DYNAMIC SIMULATION
  Trace all simulation scenarios. For each one: state the exact
  starting condition, trace the code path step by step line by
  line, identify the exact line where it fails (if any), state the
  failure mode precisely. Pay special attention to scenarios that
  have historically caused real bugs: S9-S17 (inputs), S24-S30
  (trigger/sell), S31-S36 (resume).

PHASE 4 — ROOT-CAUSE ATTRIBUTION
  For every bug found: is it a symptom or a root cause? Group
  symptoms with shared root causes. Rank by worst-case real-money
  outcome:
    CRITICAL = stranded funds or complete sell failure (no recovery)
    HIGH     = silent wrong behavior or data corruption
    MEDIUM   = recoverable with degraded experience
    LOW      = cosmetic / logging / style

PHASE 5 — IMPLEMENT FIXES
  Fix root causes, not symptoms. For each fix:
  (a) Quote the BEFORE code (exact, verbatim)
  (b) Quote the AFTER code (exact)
  (c) Add inline comment: // FIX rNN SEVERITY: [WHY, not what]
  (d) Apply Rule R8 gate questions — any YES = trace and fix fully
  (e) If this is an instance of a recurring PATTERN (A-M), explicitly
      search for all other instances of the same pattern and fix them

PHASE 6 — REGRESSION VERIFICATION
  Re-read every changed function completely. Re-run all simulation
  scenarios against the fixed code. For each fix: "Does this change
  the behavior of any code path I haven't explicitly accounted for?"
  If yes → trace and fix.

PHASE 7 — INVARIANT PROOF
  For each invariant I1-I12: verify it holds in the fixed code.
  Quote the specific line(s) that enforce it. State explicitly which
  fix restored any violated invariant.

PHASE 8 — VERSION BUMP (Rule R10, atomic)
  Bump all six version strings. Add FIX LOG entry at the TOP of
  web-api.js for every new fix applied in this pass. Update features[]
  arrays in BOTH engine-version emit sites.

PHASE 9 — ELITE FINAL PASS (Rule R11, mandatory)
  Ask: "If I received this project tomorrow with no prior context
  and ran the same audit, would I find any new bugs?"
  If YES → return to Phase 2. Repeat until genuinely NO.
  When NO: document specifically why each risk category is now closed.
  Reference specific fix IDs. Be precise enough for another engineer
  to verify without running the code.

